An announcement arrived: a push from another repo that needs your reaction and expects no answer. Pushing one yourself → `sdh-cross-repo-announce-send`; a `REQ-*.md` arrived → `sdh-cross-repo-req-answer`.

## When

At session start **and** at every release closure (a long session outlives an arrival).

## Where

- `docs/InOutBox/inbox/<sender>/` — every direction: a client reading its server, a parent server reading a microservice.
- Transition: a frozen legacy folder (`docs/server-announcements/`, `docs/microservice-announcements/`) may still hold un-migrated files — process one the same way and delete it; deletion is the old-style acknowledgement the app honours and never resurrects. Never write into those folders yourself.
- Every role → also the peer channel `docs/InOutBox/inbox/<peer>/` and `pact/<peer>/` (`sdh-peer-channel-answer`) — nothing else surfaces a peer NOTE.

## Each `ANNOUNCE-*.md`

1. Read it and apply what "What you need to do" requires.
2. An announcement introducing a new error code or status: test that exact code against your handler, even if it says "keep your current check" — an equality predicate silently routes a new code elsewhere.
3. Drop an empty marker `ANNOUNCE-NNN_<slug>.md.ack/<key>` — the key is your login on a shared repo, your repo's canonical name otherwise. That marker is the acknowledgement; the app carries it back to the sender. Anything non-empty, mis-named or nested inside `.ack/` is an issue, not an ack.
4. Commit the marker together with the changes the announcement caused.

Never delete the body — it is a replica: the mirror restores it, and without your marker the announcement never closes. The app deletes body and markers on both sides after every expected key has arrived; a new revision of the body resets the ack group, so check whether a file you already acked came back changed.
